Rules of the road
=================

1. One exported symbol per file.  src/stralloc_cats.c defines
   stralloc_cats() and nothing else that is visible outside it.
   Helpers are static.  The file is named after what it exports.

2. One header per family.  include/stralloc.h declares every
   stralloc_* function.

3. No stdio in the library.  Output goes through buffer_*, numbers
   through fmt_* and scan_*.  (Tests in t/ may use stdio.)

4. Functions return 1 for success and 0 for failure with errno set,
   unless the header says otherwise.  No hidden global state.

5. Every src/*.o is archived into libkraft.a; programs link against
   it so only what they use is pulled in.

6. Exit codes: 0 success, 100 permanent failure (bad usage, bad
   request), 111 temporary failure (network, out of memory).

7. FreeBSD first.  Use what is in base (kqueue, libedit, OpenSSL,
   Capsicum) before reaching for a port.

8. Build with FreeBSD make.  No GNU make.

Layout
------

  kraft.c     main(): options, setup, and the prompt loop
  agent/      the rest of the program, one exported function per file
  agent/agent.h   its shared state and functions
  agent/state.c   that state (data only: no functions)
  src/        the library, one function per file
  include/    the library's headers
  t/          tests; run them with make test
  man/        manual pages

The library (src/) knows nothing of the program's state: everything
it needs comes in as arguments.  The program (agent/) shares state
through agent.h; a helper used by only one function, and short, is
static in that function's file.  Program functions that would clash
with libc's names are prefixed, as with kwarn() for warn(3).

The main pieces of agent/:

  task, work     a task: the user's words, then model and tools in turn
  exchange       one request to the model, with retries (attempt)
  runtools       running the model's tool calls; approve, check (the
                 auto-mode model check), ruled and saverule (the rules)
  subagent       the task tool
  compact        replacing the conversation with a summary
  attach         images and PDFs
  save, resume, sessdir        sessions
  setundo, undo                /undo
  loadconfigs, setting, trust  config files and trusted projects
  mksystem       the system prompt, git context included
  out, raw, sgr, lines, announce, spin, ...   the terminal

How it fits together
--------------------

task() runs the agent loop: the user's task goes into the
conversation (conv_*), the backend renders the conversation into a
request, the reply streams back over HTTP (http_*, tls_*) as
server-sent events (sse_*), and the backend's event function builds
the assistant message from them.  If that message calls tools, they
run (tool_*) and their results go back as the next user message, and
round it goes until the model stops calling tools.

The conversation is append-only.  Anthropic thinking blocks are kept
exactly as received and sent back unchanged, as the API requires.

API backends
------------

Each backend is a pair of functions, PREFIX_render (conversation to
request body) and PREFIX_event (one stream event into the assistant
message), listed in the apis[] table in kraft.c:

  anth_*   Anthropic Messages API     POST /v1/messages
  oai_*    OpenAI Chat Completions    POST /v1/chat/completions

Tools
-----

Each tool is one tool_* function taking its input as a JSON object,
listed with its description and JSON Schema in tool_all.c.  Tools
marked ask need the user's approval.  A tool returns 1 on success or
0 on failure, with its output or the reason in out; either way the
model sees it.

TLS
---

tls_connect() forks a helper that owns the TLS session and relays
plaintext over a socketpair, ucspi-tls style, so the HTTP code only
ever sees a file descriptor.  It uses the OpenSSL in base.  Once the
handshake is done the helper limits its two sockets to read, write
and poll, points stdio at /dev/null, and enters Capsicum capability
mode (ps shows state C).

Secrets
-------

The model must never see the API key.  kraft blanks the key's
environment variables, in place and then with unsetenv(), as soon as
it has built its headers, so commands do not inherit them and
procstat -e shows nothing.  path_secret() keeps the file tools out of
~/.kraft, grep and find_files skip it, and perm_auto() never lets a
command that mentions .kraft run without asking.

Fuzzing
-------

t/fuzz.c is a libFuzzer harness for the code that reads untrusted
input: HTTP, SSE and JSON from the network, both stream formats,
saved sessions and config files, HTML, Markdown, the auto-mode
command rules, edits and diffs.  The first byte of an input picks
the target; some targets check properties (a session saved and
loaded again is the same; Markdown cut anywhere renders the same;
json_str and json_unstr are inverses) and abort if one fails.

  make fuzz
  mkdir -p corpus && cp t/corpus/* corpus/
  ./t/fuzz -max_total_time=600 -max_len=60000 corpus

It is built from the library's sources with the address and
undefined-behaviour sanitizers, using the clang in base.
